@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@center @image{images/buttercup,,,Ranculus repens, photo by sannse,jpg}

Buttercup is a behavior-driven development framework for testing Emacs
Lisp code.  It allows to group related tests so they can share common
set-up and tear-down code, and allows the programmer to “spy” on
functions to ensure they are called with the right arguments during
testing.

The framework is heavily inspired by
@url{https://jasmine.github.io/edge/introduction.html,Jasmine}.

@c TODO: Use a
@heading Example
@c @node Example
@c @section Example

A simple test looks like this.  @strong{Please notice} that
@code{lexical-binding: t} is @emph{required} in files defining
buttercup tests.

@lisp
;;; lexical-binding is required -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(describe "A suite"
  (it "contains a spec with an expectation"
    (expect t :to-be t)))
@end lisp

@node Installation and basic use
@chapter Installation and basic use

Install buttercup from @url{https://elpa.nongnu.org,NonGNU ELPA}.  It
is also available in @url{https://stable.melpa.org,MELPA Stable} or in a
bleeding edge version from @url{https://melpa.org,MELPA}.  There is
usually no reason to use the bleeding edge version.  New stable
versions are released soon after major changes or bugfixes.

Tests are usually defined in Emacs Lisp files with a @samp{test-}
prefix, like @samp{test-feature.el}.  Test files are usually
structured as follows

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)
(describe "A suite"
  (it "contains a spec with an expectation"
    (expect t :to-be t))
  (it "contains another spec without an expectation"))
(describe "Another suite, without specs")
@end lisp 

Buttercup tests can be run in many ways, but a good way to start is
with the @command{buttercup} command included in the package.

@example
$ buttercup -L .
Running 1 out of 2 specs.

A suite
  contains a spec with an expectation (4.59ms)
  contains another spec without an expectation  PENDING (0.03ms)

Another suite, without specs

Ran 1 out of 2 specs, 0 failed, in 11.07ms.
@end example

@node Writing tests
@chapter Writing tests

Buttercup is a behavior-driven development framework for testing Emacs
Lisp code.  It does not depend on any other Emacs Lisp libraries.  It
has a clean, obvious syntax so that you can easily write tests.

It is heavily inspired by @url{https://jasmine.github.io/,Jasmine}.
So heavily inspired, in fact, that most of this page is more or less a
verbatim copy of the
@url{https://jasmine.github.io/edge/introduction.html,Jasmine
introduction}.

@c All code in this file can be run by Buttercup’s built-in markdown test
@c runner. Just use `make test` in the project directory to see the
@c output.

@menu
* Suites::
* Specs::
@end menu

@node Suites
@section Suites: ``describe'' your tests

A test suite begins with a call to the Buttercup macro @code{describe}
with the first parameter being a string describing the suite and the
rest being the body of code that implements the suite.

@strong{Please notice} that @code{lexical-binding: t} is
@emph{required} in files defining buttercup tests.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)
(describe "A suite"
  (it "contains a spec with an expectation"
    (expect t :to-be t)))
@end lisp

@node Specs
@section Specs

Specs are defined by calling the Buttercup macro @code{it}, which like
@code{describe} takes a string and code.  The string is the @var{title} of
the spec and the @var{code} is the spec, or test.  A spec contains one or
more @var{expectations} that test the state of the code.  An expectation in
Buttercup is an assertion that is either true or false.  A spec with
all true expectations is a passing spec.  A spec with one or more false
expectations is a failing spec.

