@node It's just functions
@subsection It's just functions

The code arguments to @code{describe} and @code{it} is just turned into
functions internally, so they can contain any executable code
necessary to implement the rules. Emacs Lisp scoping rules apply, so
make sure to define your spec file to be lexically scoped.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)
(describe "A suite is just a function"
  :var (a)
  (it "and so is a spec"
    (setq a t)
    (expect a :to-be t)))
@end lisp

@node Expectations
@subsection Expectations

Expectations are expressed with the @code{expect} macro.  Its first
argument is the actual value.  The second argument is a test keyword,
followed by expected values for the test to compare the actual value
against.

If there is no test, the argument is simply tested for being non-nil.
This can be used by people who dislike the matcher syntax or when
there is no suitable matcher.

@menu
* Matchers::
* Included matchers::
@end menu

@node Matchers
@subsubsection Matchers

Each matcher implements a boolean comparison between the actual value
and the expected value.  It is responsible for reporting to Buttercup
if the expectation is true or false.  Buttercup will then pass or fail
the spec.

Any matcher can evaluate to a negative assertion by prepending it with
the @code{:not} matcher.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)
(describe "The :to-be matcher compares with `eq'"
  (it "and has a positive case"
    (expect t :to-be t))
  (it "and can have a negative case"
    (expect nil :not :to-be t)))
@end lisp

@node Included matchers
@subsubsection Included matchers

Buttercup has a rich set of matchers included.  Each is used in the
example below --- all expectations and specs pass.  There is also the
ability to write custom matchers (see the
@code{buttercup-define-matcher} macro for further information) for
when a project’s domain calls for specific assertions that are not
included below.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)

(describe "Included matchers:"
  (it "The :to-be matcher compares with `eq'"
    (let* ((a 12)
           (b a))
      (expect a :to-be b)
      (expect a :not :to-be nil)))

  (describe "The :to-equal matcher"
    (it "works for simple literals and variables"
      (let ((a 12))
        (expect a :to-equal 12)))

    (it "should work for compound objects"
      (let ((foo '((a . 12) (b . 34)))
            (bar '((a . 12) (b . 34))))
        (expect foo :to-equal bar))))

  (it "The :to-have-same-items-as matcher compares two lists as sets"
    (let ((first (list "a" "b" "c"))
          (second (list "c" "a" "b"))
          (third (list "a" "c" "d"))
          (fourth (list "a" "b")))
      (expect first :to-have-same-items-as second)
      (expect second :to-have-same-items-as first)
      (expect first :not :to-have-same-items-as third)
      (expect third :not :to-have-same-items-as second)
      (expect first :not :to-have-same-items-as fourth)
      (expect fourth :not :to-have-same-items-as first)))

  (it "The :to-match matcher is for regular expressions"
    (let ((message "foo bar baz"))
      (expect message :to-match "bar")
      (expect message :to-match (rx "bar"))
      (expect message :not :to-match "quux")))

  (it "The :to-be-truthy matcher is for boolean casting testing"
    (let (a
          (foo "foo"))
      (expect foo :to-be-truthy)
      (expect a :not :to-be-truthy)))

  (it "The :to-contain matcher is for finding an item in a list"
    (let ((a '("foo" "bar" "baz")))
      (expect a :to-contain "bar")
      (expect a :not :to-contain "quux")))

  (it "The :to-be-less-than matcher is for mathematical comparisons"
    (let ((pi 3.1415926)
          (e 2.78))
      (expect e :to-be-less-than pi)
      (expect pi :not :to-be-less-than e)))

  (it "The :to-be-greater-than matcher is for mathematical comparisons"
    (let ((pi 3.1415926)
          (e 2.78))
      (expect pi :to-be-greater-than e)
      (expect e :not :to-be-greater-than pi)))

  (it "The :to-be-close-to matcher is for precision math comparison"
    (let ((pi 3.1415926)
          (e 2.78))
      (expect pi :not :to-be-close-to e 2)
      (expect pi :to-be-close-to e 0)))

  (describe "The :to-throw matcher"
    (it "is for testing if an expression throws an exception"
      (expect (+ 1 2) :not :to-throw)
      (expect (+ a 1) :to-throw))
    (it "accepts a symbol to check for the signal thrown"
      (expect (/ 1 0) :not :to-throw 'void-variable)
      (expect (+ a 1) :to-throw 'void-variable))
    (it "optionally matches arguments to signals"
        (expect (+ a 1) :not :to-throw 'void-variable '(b))
        (expect (+ a 1) :to-throw 'void-variable '(a)))
	(it "matches inherited signals"
	  (expect (signal 'overflow-error nil) :to-throw 'overflow-error)
	  (expect (signal 'overflow-error nil) :to-throw 'arith-error)
	  (expect (signal 'overflow-error nil) :to-throw 'error)
	  (expect (signal 'arith-error nil) :not :to-throw 'overflow-error)
	  (expect (signal 'arith-error nil) :to-throw 'arith-error)
	  (expect (signal 'arith-error nil) :to-throw 'error))))
@end lisp

If you are migrating from @samp{ERT}, you can also use @code{should}
and similar macros inside a buttercup test just like you would inside
an @code{ert-deftest} form.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'ert)
(require 'buttercup)

(describe "ERT support"
  (it "allows you to use ERT macros in tests"
    (let* ((a 12)
           (b a))
      (should (= a b))
      (should-not (eq a nil))
      (should-error (error "Throws an error")))))
@end lisp

@node Grouping Related Specs with @code{describe}
@subsection Grouping Related Specs with @code{describe}

The @code{describe} macro is for grouping related specs.  The string
parameter is for naming the collection of specs, and will be
concatenated with specs to make a spec’s full name.  This aids in
finding specs in a large suite.  If you name them well, your specs
read as full sentences in traditional
@url{http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Behavior-driven_development,@acronym{BDD}}
style.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)
(describe "A spec"
  (it "is just a function, so it can contain any code"
    (let ((foo 0))
      (setq foo (1+ foo))

      (expect foo :to-equal 1)))

  (it "can have more than one expectation"
    (let ((foo 0))
      (setq foo (1+ foo))

      (expect foo :to-equal 1)
      (expect t :to-equal t))))
@end lisp


@menu
* Declaring variables::
* Setup and Teardown::
* Nesting @code{describe} Blocks::
@end menu

@node Declaring variables
@subsubsection Declaring Variables

The @code{describe} macro supports the optional @code{:var} and
@code{:var*} args.  These bind variables for the suite by passing them
as a @samp{varlist} to the @code{let} and @code{let*} form
respectively.  Only one instance of @code{:var} or @code{:var*} is
allowed, and it must come first in the @code{describe} form.  It can
not be interspersed between @code{it} statements.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)
(describe "A spec using :VAR"
  :var ((foo 1))
  (it "has access to the variables bound in :VAR"
    (expect foo :to-be 1)))

(describe "A spec using :VAR*"
  :var* ((foo 1)
        (bar (1+ foo)))
  (it "has access to the variables bound in :VAR* which can refer \
to symbols already bound"
    (expect bar :to-be 2)))
@end lisp

@c It's important to note that @code{lexical-binding} must be
@c @samp{non-nil} for @code{:var} and @code{:var*} to work properly.
@c Within a test file this is usually set using a local file variable.

Using @code{:var} and @code{:var*} works just like the @code{let}
equivalents, but it's recommended to use the @code{:var} format to be
future proof.  Future internal changes in buttercup could break suites
using @code{let}.


@node Setup and Teardown
@subsubsection Setup and Teardown

To help a test suite
@url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don%27t_repeat_yourself,@acronym{DRY}} up any
duplicated setup and teardown code, buttercup provides the
@code{before-each}, @code{after-each}, @code{before-all} and
@code{after-all} special forms.

As the name implies, code blocks defined with @code{before-each} are
called once before each spec in the @code{describe} is run, and the
@code{after-each} code blocks are called once after each spec.

Here is the same set of specs written a little differently. The
variable under test is defined at the top-level scope --- the
@code{describe} block --- and initialization code is moved into a
@code{before-each} block.  The @code{after-each} block resets the
variable before continuing.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)

(describe "A spec using `before-each' and `after-each'"
  :var (foo)
  (before-each
   (when (not foo)
     (setq foo 0))
   (setq foo (1+ foo)))

  (after-each
   (setq foo 0))

  (it "is just a function, so it can contain any code"
    (expect foo :to-equal 1))

  (it "can have more than one expectation"
    (expect foo :to-equal 1)
    (expect t :to-equal t)))
@end lisp

The @code{before-all} form is called only once before all the specs in
@code{describe} are run, and the @code{after-all} form is called after
all specs finish.  These functions can be used to speed up test suites
with expensive setup and teardown.

However, be careful using @code{before-all} and @code{after-all}!
Since they are not reset between specs, it is easy to accidentally
leak state between your specs so that they erroneously pass or fail.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)

(describe "A spec using `before-all' and `after-all'"
  :var (foo)
  (before-all
   (setq foo 1))

  (after-all
   (setq foo 0))

  (it "sets the initial value of foo before specs run"
    (expect foo :to-equal 1)
    (setq foo (1+ foo)))

  (it "does not reset foo between specs"
    (expect foo :to-equal 2)))
@end lisp

@node Nesting @code{describe} Blocks
@subsubsection Nesting @code{describe} Blocks

Calls to @code{describe} can be nested, with specs defined at any level.
This allows a suite to be composed as a tree of functions. Before a
spec is executed, buttercup walks down the tree executing each
@code{before-each} function in order. After the spec is executed, buttercup
walks through the @code{after-each} functions similarly.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)
(describe "A spec"
  :var (foo)
  (before-each
   (setq foo 0)
   (setq foo (1+ foo)))

  (after-each
   (setq foo 0))

  (it "is just a function, so it can contain any code"
    (expect foo :to-equal 1))

  (it "can have more than one expectation"
    (expect foo :to-equal 1)
    (expect t :to-equal t))

  (describe "nested inside a second describe"
    (let (bar)
      (before-each
       (setq bar 1))

      (it "can reference both scopes as needed"
        (expect foo :to-equal bar)))))
@end lisp

@node Disabling Suites
@subsection Disabling Suites

Suites and specs can be disabled by marking them as pending with the
@code{xdescribe} and @code{xit} macros, respectively.  Any suites or
specs inside a @code{xdescribe} suite is also pending.  Pending suites and
specs will be listed as pending in the results, but the containing
code will not be run.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)

(xdescribe "A spec"
  :var (foo)
  (before-each
    (setq foo 0)
    (setq foo (1+ foo)))

  (it "is just a function, so it can contain any code"
    (expect foo :to-equal 1)))
@end lisp

@node Pending Specs
@subsection Pending Specs

Pending specs do not run, but will be listed in the results.

Any spec declared with @code{xit} is marked as pending.

Any spec declared without a function body will also be marked as
pending in results.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)

(describe "Pending specs"
  (xit "can be declared using `xit'"
    (expect t :to-be nil))

  (it "can be declared with `it' but without a body"))
@end lisp


@node Conditionally Skipping Specs
@subsection Conditionally Skipping Specs

Use the @code{assume} macro to conditionally skip a spec.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)

(describe "Conditionally skip specs"
  (it "with the `assume' macro"
    (assume (fboundp 'new-function) "`new-function' not availeble")
    (expect (new-function))))
@end lisp

If the first argument to @code{assume} evals to @code{nil}, the spec
will be marked as pending, and the second arg @var{message} will be
added to the output.

@node Spies
@subsection Spies

Buttercup has test double functions called spies.  While other
frameworks call these mocks and similar, we call them spies, because
their main job is to spy in on function calls.  Also, Jasmine calls
them spies, and so do we.  A spy can stub any function --- whether it
already exists or not --- and tracks calls to it and all arguments.

Spies may only be created in @code{before-each} or @code{it} blocks.
Spies are removed and all counters reset after each spec and its
@code{after-each} blocks have completed.  There are special matchers
for interacting with spies.  The @code{:to-have-been-called} matcher
will return @samp{non-nil} if the spy was called at all.  The
@code{:to-have-been-called-with} matcher will return true if the
argument list matches any of the recorded calls to the spy.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)

(describe "A spy"
  :var (foo bar)
  (before-each
   (setf (symbol-function 'foo)
         (lambda (value)
           (setq bar value)))

   (spy-on 'foo)

   (foo 123)
   (foo 456 "another param"))

  (it "tracks that the spy was called"
    (expect 'foo :to-have-been-called)
    (foo 789))

  (it "resets tracking after each spec"
    (expect 'foo :not :to-have-been-called-with 789))

  (describe "that is defined in a nested `describe'"
    (before-each
      (spy-on 'foo :and-return-value 1))
    (it "will override any outer spy"
      (expect (foo 789) :to-equal 1)
      (expect 'foo :not :to-have-been-called-with 123)))

  (it "will not be active outside it's scope"
    (expect (foo 789) :to-equal nil))

  (it "tracks all arguments of its calls"
    (expect 'foo :to-have-been-called-with 123)
    (expect 'foo :to-have-been-called-with 456 "another param"))

  (it "stops all execution on a function"
    (expect bar :to-be nil)))
@end lisp

The @code{:to-have-been-called-times} matcher will return true if the
spy was called a certain number of times.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)

(describe "A spy"
  :var (foo bar)
  (before-each
   (setf (symbol-function 'foo)
         (lambda (value)
           (setq bar value)))

   (spy-on 'foo)

   (foo 123)
   (foo 456 "another param"))

  (it "tracks that the spy was called twice"
    (expect 'foo :to-have-been-called-times 2)))
@end lisp

