@node Spies and-call-through
@subsubsection Spies: @code{:and-call-through}

The keyword argument @code{:and-call-through} to @code{spy-on} will
make the spy call the original function instead of returning
@code{nil}.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'buttercup)

(describe "A spy, when configured to call through"
  :var (bar set-bar get-bar fetched-bar)
  (before-each
    (fset 'set-bar (lambda (val)
                     (setq bar val)))
    (fset 'get-bar (lambda ()
                     bar))

    (spy-on 'get-bar :and-call-through)

    (set-bar 123)
    (setq fetched-bar (get-bar)))

  (it "tracks that the spy was called"
    (expect 'get-bar :to-have-been-called))

  (it "should not affect other functions"
    (expect bar :to-equal 123))

  (it "when called returns the requested value"
    (expect fetched-bar :to-equal 123)))
@end lisp

@node and-return-value
@subsubsection Spies: @code{:and-return-value}

The keyword argument @code{:and-return-value} specifies the value the
spied-on function should return.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(describe "A spy, when configured to fake a return value"
  :var (bar set-bar get-bar fetched-bar)
  (before-each
    (fset 'set-bar (lambda (val)
                     (setq bar val)))
    (fset 'get-bar (lambda ()
                     bar))

    (spy-on 'get-bar :and-return-value 745)

    (set-bar 123)
    (setq fetched-bar (get-bar)))

  (it "tracks that the spy was called"
    (expect 'get-bar :to-have-been-called))

  (it "should not affect other functions"
    (expect bar :to-equal 123))

  (it "when called returns the requested value"
    (expect fetched-bar :to-equal 745)))
@end lisp


@node and-call-fake
@subsubsection Spies: @code{:and-call-fake}

The keyword argument @code{:and-call-fake} delegates calls to a
supplied function.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(describe "A spy, when configured with an alternate implementation"
  :var (bar set-bar get-bar fetched-bar)
  (before-each
    (fset 'set-bar (lambda (val)
                     (setq bar val)))
    (fset 'get-bar (lambda ()
                     bar))

    (spy-on 'get-bar :and-call-fake (lambda () 1001))

    (set-bar 123)
    (setq fetched-bar (get-bar)))

  (it "tracks that the spy was called"
    (expect 'get-bar :to-have-been-called))

  (it "should not affect other functions"
    (expect bar :to-equal 123))

  (it "when called returns the requested value"
    (expect fetched-bar :to-equal 1001)))
@end lisp

@node and-throw-error
@subsubsection Spies: @code{:and-throw-error}

With the keyword argument @code{:and-throw-error}, all calls to the
spy will @code{signal} the specified value as an error.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(describe "A spy, when configured to throw an error"
  :var (bar set-bar get-bar fetched-bar)
  (before-each
    (fset 'set-bar (lambda (val)
                     (setq bar val)))
    (fset 'get-bar (lambda ()
                     bar))

    (spy-on 'get-bar :and-throw-error 'error))

  (it "throws the error"
    (expect (get-bar)
            :to-throw 'error)))
@end lisp

@node Other tracking properties
@subsubsection Other tracking properties

Every call to a spy is tracked and exposed using the @code{spy-calls}
accessor. This tracks both successful calls and calls that throw
errors.

@code{spy-calls-any} returns @code{nil} if the spy has not been called
at all, and then @code{t} once at least one call happens.
@code{spy-calls-count} returns the number of times the spy was called.
@code{spy-calls-args-for} returns the arguments passed to a given call
(by index).  @code{spy-calls-all-args} returns the arguments to all
calls.  @code{spy-calls-all} returns the context (current buffer,
arguments passed and return status) of all calls.
@code{spy-calls-most-recent} returns the context of the most recent
call.  @code{spy-calls-first} returns the context for the first call.

Contexts are represented by instances of the @code{spy-context} struct with
the slots @code{args}, @code{current-buffer}, @code{return-value} and
@code{thrown-signal}.  The @code{return-value} and @code{thrown-signal} slots
represent the return status.  Calling @code{spy-context-return-value} for a
context representing a raised signal (or vice versa) will raise an
error.  Test the context type with @code{spy-context-return-p} and
@code{spy-context-thrown-p}.

Finally, @code{spy-calls-reset} clears all tracking for a spy.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(describe "A spy"
  :var (set-foo foo)
  (before-each
    (fset 'set-foo (lambda (val &rest ignored)
                     (setq foo val)))
    (spy-on 'set-foo))

  (it "tracks if it was called at all"
    (expect (spy-calls-any 'set-foo)
            :to-equal
            nil)

    (set-foo 5)

    (expect (spy-calls-any 'set-foo)
            :to-equal
            t))

  (it "tracks the number of times it was called"
    (expect (spy-calls-count 'set-foo)
            :to-equal
            0)

    (set-foo 2)
    (set-foo 3)

    (expect (spy-calls-count 'set-foo)
            :to-equal
            2))

  (it "tracks the arguments of each call"
    (set-foo 123)
    (set-foo 456 "baz")

    (expect (spy-calls-args-for 'set-foo 0)
            :to-equal
            '(123))

    (expect (spy-calls-args-for 'set-foo 1)
            :to-equal
            '(456 "baz")))

  (it "tracks the arguments of all calls"
    (set-foo 123)
    (set-foo 456 "baz")

    (expect (spy-calls-all-args 'set-foo)
            :to-equal
            '((123)
              (456 "baz"))))

  (it "can provide the context and arguments to all calls"
    (set-foo 123)

    (expect (spy-calls-all 'set-foo)
            :to-equal
            `(,(make-spy-context :current-buffer (current-buffer)
                                 :args '(123)))))

  (it "has a shortcut to the most recent call"
    (set-foo 123)
    (set-foo 456 "baz")

    (expect (spy-calls-most-recent 'set-foo)
            :to-equal
            (make-spy-context :current-buffer (current-buffer)
                              :args '(456 "baz"))))

  (it "has a shortcut to the first call"
    (set-foo 123)
    (set-foo 456 "baz")

    (expect (spy-calls-first 'set-foo)
            :to-equal
            (make-spy-context :current-buffer (current-buffer)
                              :args '(123))))

  (it "tracks the return values and error signals of each call"
    ;; Set up `set-foo' so that it can either return a value or throw
    ;; an error
    (spy-on 'set-foo :and-call-fake
            (lambda (val &rest ignored)
              (if (>= val 0)
                  val
                (error "Value must not be negative"))))
    (expect (set-foo 1) :to-be 1)
    (expect (set-foo -1) :to-throw 'error)
    (expect (spy-context-return-p (spy-calls-first 'set-foo)))
    (expect
     (spy-context-return-value (spy-calls-first 'set-foo))
     :to-be 1)
    ;; Trying to get the thrown signal from a call that didn't throw a
    ;; signal is an error
    (expect
     (spy-context-thrown-signal
      (spy-calls-first 'set-foo))
     :to-throw)

    (expect (spy-context-thrown-p (spy-calls-most-recent 'set-foo)))
    (expect
     (spy-context-thrown-signal
      (spy-calls-most-recent 'set-foo))
     :to-equal '(error "Value must not be negative"))
    ;; Trying to get the return value from a call that threw a signal
    ;; raises an error
    (expect
     (spy-context-return-value
      (spy-calls-most-recent 'set-foo))
     :to-throw)
    ;; Use :return-value and :thrown-signal
    ;; to create matching spy-contexts
    (expect
     (spy-calls-all 'set-foo)
      :to-equal
      (list
       (make-spy-context :args '(1)
                         :current-buffer (current-buffer)
                         :return-value 1)
       (make-spy-context
         :args '(-1)
         :current-buffer (current-buffer)
         :thrown-signal '(error "Value must not be negative")))))

  (it "counts the number of successful and failed calls"
    ;; Set up `set-foo' so that it can either return a value or throw
    ;; an error
    (spy-on 'set-foo :and-call-fake
            (lambda (val &rest ignored)
              (if (>= val 0)
                  val
                (error "Value must not be negative"))))
    (expect (set-foo 1) :to-be 1)
    (expect (set-foo 2) :to-be 2)
    (expect (set-foo 3) :to-be 3)
    (expect (set-foo -1) :to-throw 'error)
    (expect (set-foo -2) :to-throw 'error)
    (expect (set-foo -3) :to-throw 'error)
    (expect (set-foo -4) :to-throw 'error)

    (expect (spy-calls-count 'set-foo)
            :to-be 7)
    (expect (spy-calls-count-returned 'set-foo)
            :to-be 3)
    (expect (spy-calls-count-errors 'set-foo)
            :to-be 4))

  (it "can be reset"
    (set-foo 123)
    (set-foo 456 "baz")

    (expect (spy-calls-any 'set-foo)
            :to-be
            t)

    (spy-calls-reset 'set-foo)

    (expect (spy-calls-any 'set-foo)
            :to-be
            nil)))
@end lisp

@node Warnings in tests
@subsection Warnings in tests

By default, buttercup captures any warning emitted during a test and
displays them all after the test completes in order to keep the output
readable.  If you need to suppress this (for example if your test
deals with the warnings itself), you can use the macro
@code{buttercup-suppress-warning-capture}, which works like
@code{progn} but suppresses buttercup's warning capturing within the
body.

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(describe "A test"
  (it "can issue warnings while running"
    (display-warning
      'buttercup
      "This warning should be visible after the test report.")
    (expect (+ 2 2) :to-equal 4))

  (it "can capture its own warnings as part of the test"
    (buttercup-suppress-warning-capture
      (let ((warning-text
             (format
               "This warning, issued at %s should be sent to the *Warnings* buffer as normal."
               (current-time-string))))
        (display-warning 'buttercup warning-text)
        (expect (with-current-buffer "*Warnings*"
                  (buffer-string))
                :to-match (regexp-quote warning-text))))))
@end lisp


@node Running tests
@chapter Running tests

@menu
* Usage::
@end menu

@node Usage
@section Usage

Buttercup is primarily meant to be used non-interactively, to
automatically test a project independent of a user’s setup, before a
commit and on a continuous integration platform.  Because of this, the
recommended workflow for buttercup does not use interactive commands
but instead the command line.

@menu
* Cask::
* Eldev::
* Eask::
* Projectile::
* Travis::
@end menu

@node Cask
@subsection Cask

@url{https://github.com/cask/cask,Cask} is a project to create an
environment separate from your usual interactive Emacs environment.
This allows you to install the packages your project depends on, and
only those, and test your project in a well-defined environment.

Buttercup works best in such environments, so the following best
practices rely on Cask to be installed.

@menu
* Cask directory layout::
* Running tests with Cask::
@end menu

@node Cask directory layout
@subsubsection Cask directory layout

A basic project layout requires a project file, called @file{feature.el}
here, a @file{Cask} file to define dependencies, and a @file{tests/} directory
for tests.  It should look roughly like this:

@example
feature/feature.el
        Cask
        tests/test-feature.el
@end example

@subsubheading @file{feature.el}

@lisp
(defun featurize (bug feature)
  (format "It's not a %s, it's a %s" bug feature))

(provide 'feature)
@end lisp

@subsubheading @file{Cask}

@lisp
(source gnu)
(source melpa-stable)

(development
 (depends-on "buttercup"))
@end lisp

@subsubheading @file{tests/test-feature.el}

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'feature)

(describe "The feature"
  (it "can use bug and feature"
    (expect (featurize "bug" "feature")
            :to-equal
            "It's not a bug, it's a feature")))
@end lisp

@node Running tests with Cask
@subsubsection Running tests with Cask

You can now use Cask to run your tests.

First, you have to install the dependencies. You only have to do this
once, or when the dependencies change:

@example
$ @command{cask}
Extracting buttercup-1.1/
Extracting buttercup-1.1/buttercup-compat.el
Extracting buttercup-1.1/buttercup.el
Extracting buttercup-1.1/bin/
Extracting buttercup-1.1/bin/buttercup
Extracting buttercup-1.1/buttercup-pkg.el
Generating autoloads for buttercup-compat.el...
Generating autoloads for buttercup-compat.el...done
Generating autoloads for buttercup-pkg.el...
Generating autoloads for buttercup-pkg.el...done
Generating autoloads for buttercup.el...
Generating autoloads for buttercup.el...done
@end example

Now, you can run your tests:

@example
$ @command{cask} exec buttercup -L .
Running 1 specs.

The feature
  can use bug and feature

Ran 1 specs, 0 failed, in 0.0 seconds.
@end example

That’s it. Buttercup’s built-in discover test runner looks for files
named @file{test-*.el}, @file{*-test.el} or @file{*-tests.el}.

Use the @option{--pattern} @var{PATTERN} option to only run tests with
names matching @var{PATTERN}.  The @option{--pattern} option can be
used multiple times, in which case tests will be run if they match any
of the given patterns.  Combine with the @option{--no-skip} option to
filter out the skipped tests.

You can run this command whichever way you like.  Common choices
include a makefile or shell scripts.

@node Eldev
@subsection Eldev

@url{https://github.com/doublep/eldev,Eldev} is another Elisp build tool
and has built-in support for Buttercup.  Like Cask, it installs
dependencies of your project automatically, ensuring consistent
environment.

@menu
* Eldev directory layout::
* Running tests with Eldev::
@end menu

@node Eldev directory layout
@subsubsection Eldev directory layout

Typical project layout used for Cask also works for Eldev.  However,
the tool is very liberal and with a couple of lines you can configure
it to handle almost any layout a project could have (see the
@url{https://github.com/doublep/eldev#testing,Eldev documentation} if
needed).

Unlike Cask, however, Eldev needs that your test files include form
@code{(require 'buttercup)}.  Otherwise you will get Elisp errors like
@samp{“Symbol’s function definition is void: describe”}.  Basically, Eldev
requires that the @file{.el} files are @emph{self-contained} and do not depend
on certain external tool.

@node Running tests with Eldev
@subsubsection Running tests with Eldev

The tool installs project dependencies automatically when needed, so
you don’t have to bother about that.  All you need to do is to ensure
the main file of the project correctly declares them.

You also don’t need to declare that the project uses Buttercup for
testing: Eldev will determine that on-the-fly.

To run your test, just execute:

@example
$ @command{eldev} test
[1/1] Installing package ‘buttercup’ (1.23) from ‘melpa-stable’...
Running 1 specs.

The feature
  can use bug and feature (2.58ms)

Ran 1 specs, 0 failed, in 2.68ms.
@end example

You can also specify patterns on the command line, to avoid running
all the tests at once, i.e.:

@example
$ @command{eldev} test foo
@end example

If you have several test files, you can avoid running all the tests by
specifying filename after @option{-f} (@option{--file}) option:

@example
$ @command{eldev} test @option{-f} @file{main.el}
@end example

For more information, please see tool’s own documentation, especially
the section about
@url{https://github.com/doublep/eldev#testing,testing}.

@node Eask
@subsection Eask
Eask is very similar to Cask (@pxref{Cask}); anything that applies to Cask will apply to Eask

@url{https://github.com/emacs-eask/cli,Eask} is an actively maintained
replacement for Cask.

@menu
* Eask directory layout::
* Running tests with Eask::
@end menu

@node Eask directory layout
@subsubsection Eask project directory layout

The project directory layout is similar to Cask (@pxref{Cask directory
layout}). But instead of @file{Cask}-file, you should have @file{Eask}-file as
a replacement.

A basic project layout requires a project file, called @file{feature.el}
here, an @file{Eask}-file to define dependencies, and a @file{tests/} directory
for tests. It should look roughly like this:

@example
feature/feature.el
        Eask
        tests/test-feature.el
@end example

@subsubheading @file{feature.el}

@lisp
(defun featurize (bug feature)
  (format "It's not a %s, it's a %s" bug feature))

(provide 'feature)
@end lisp

@subsubheading @file{Eask}

@lisp
(source 'gnu)
(source 'melpa-stable)

(development
 (depends-on "buttercup"))
@end lisp

@subsubheading @file{tests/test-feature.el}

@lisp
;;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-
(require 'feature)

(describe "The feature"
  (it "can use bug and feature"
    (expect (featurize "bug" "feature")
            :to-equal
            "It's not a bug, it's a feature")))
@end lisp

@node Running tests with Eask
@subsubsection Running tests with Eask

You can now use Eask to run your tests.

First, you have to install the dependencies. You only have to do this once, or
when the dependencies change:

@example
$ @command{eask} install-deps @option{--dev}

Installing 1 development dependency...
Loading package information... done
  - Installing buttercup (20230119.2337)... done
(Total of 1 dependency installed, 0 skipped)
@end example

Now, you can run your tests:

@example
$ @command{eask} exec buttercup @option{-L} .
...

Running 1 specs.

The feature
  can use bug and feature

Ran 1 specs, 0 failed, in 0.0 seconds.
@end example

That’s it.  Buttercup’s built-in discover test runner looks for files
named @file{test-*.el}, @file{*-test.el} or @file{*-tests.el}.

Use the @option{--pattern} @var{PATTERN} option to only run tests with
names matching @var{PATTERN}.  The @option{--pattern} option can be
used multiple times, in which case tests will be run if they match any
of the given patterns. Combine with the @option{--no-skip} option to
filter out the skipped tests.

You can run this command whichever way you like. Common choices
include a Makefile or shell scripts.

See the @url{https://emacs-eask.github.io/,Eask documentation} on
Eask's own page for more information.

@node Projectile
@subsection Projectile

If you use @url{https://github.com/bbatsov/projectile,Projectile} for
interacting with your projects you can set the "default" project test
command to be available when you invoke @code{projectile-test-project}.
Create a @file{.dir-locals.el} file in the the root of your project tree
(next to your @file{Cask} file).  An example:

@subsubheading @file{.dir-locals.el}

@lisp
((nil . ((eval . (progn
                   (require 'projectile)
                   (puthash (projectile-project-root)
                            "cask exec buttercup -L ."
                            projectile-test-cmd-map))))))
@end lisp

If you are using Eldev as build tool, Projectile should provide
testing command on its own, so you don’t need any special steps.

@node Travis
@subsection Travis

@menu
* Cask on Travis::
* Eldev on Travis::
* Eask on Travis::
@end menu

@node Cask on Travis
@subsubsection Cask on Travis

If your project is hosted on github, you can use
@url{https://travis-ci.org/,Travis CI} as your continuous integration
environment.  Buttercup can easily be used in such a setup.  Simply add
the following @file{.travis.yml} file:

@example
language: emacs-lisp
sudo: false
cache: apt
env:
  - EVM_EMACS=emacs-24.5-travis
  - EVM_EMACS=emacs-25.1-travis
before_install: |
  curl -fsSkL https://gist.github.com/rejeep/ebcd57c3af83b049833b/raw \
    >travis.sh &&
    source ./travis.sh
  evm install $EVM_EMACS --use --skip
  cask
install:
  - cask install
script:
  - emacs --version
  - cask exec buttercup -L .
@end example

Most of the complexity here is from installing
@url{https://github.com/rejeep/evm,EVM} and Cask to be able to test
your project using different Emacs versions.

@node Eldev on Travis
@subsubsection Eldev on Travis

For Eldev, use the following @file{.travis.yml} file:

@example
language: emacs-lisp
dist: trusty

env:
  # Add more lines like this if you want to test
  # on different Emacs versions.
  - EVM_EMACS=emacs-26.3-travis

install: |
  curl -fsSL https://raw.github.com/doublep/eldev/master/webinstall/travis-eldev-and-evm \
    >x.sh &&
    source ./x.sh
  evm install $EVM_EMACS --use

script:
  - eldev -p -dtT test
@end example

For details, see the
@url{https://github.com/doublep/eldev#continuous-integration,Eldev CI documentation}.

@node Eask on Travis
@subsubsection Eask on Travis

For Eask, please see the
@url{https://emacs-eask.github.io/Continuous-Integration/Travis-CI/,
Eask Travis documentation}


